\par\par \b \uc2  Asian  \highlight22\uc2 Currencies \highlight\uc2  Lower Early; Rupiah Sets Bearish Tone \b0\par\par\uc2 485 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 Mai 2000\par 04:45\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2000, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE -(Dow Jones)- Most Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  are lower early Monday, pressured by persistent weakness in the Indonesian rupiah and the likelihood of higher U.S. interest rates. \par \par \uc2  The rupiah declined amid a continuing string of negative news coming out Jakarta, including a report that Indonesia's top economic minister threatened to resign over his frustration with the lack of progress on economic reforms. \par \par \uc2  Senior Economics Minister Kwik Kian Gie said bureaucratic red tape was hampering the country's efforts to comply with the reforms sought by the International Monetary Fund as part of its loan program with Indonesia, the Financial Times reported over the weekend. \par \par \uc2  Additionally, clashes between police and angry mobs of people in Jakarta's Chinatown Saturday dented sentiment in the rupiah, dealers said. The clashes began when officials tried to clear the sidewalks of the many vendors who gather there each day to sell items such as pirated video compact discs, police said. \par \par \uc2  At 0230 GMT (10:30 p.m. EDT Sunday), the dollar was quoted at 8,600 rupiah, up from IDR8,485 late Friday. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the dollar could rise as high as IDR8,800 Monday if Bank \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia\b0 \highlight\uc2  continues to refrain from \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support the rupiah. \par \par \uc2  The sinking rupiah, along with expectations that Federal Reserve policy makers will raise short-term rates by half a percentage point when they meet Tuesday, pushed the Singapore dollar lower. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. dollar was quoted at S$1.7273, up from S$1.7243 late Friday. Dealers said they expected the pair to test S$1.7300, but the threat of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by monetary authorities would prevent the U.S. dollar from going much above that. \par \par \uc2  Prospects for a widening interest rate differential with the U.S., the ongoing Mindanao hostage crisis and weak first-quarter agriculture production data are weighing on the Philippine peso. \par \par \uc2  The dollar was quoted at 41.575 pesos, up from PHP41.500 late Friday. Dealers said the pair could test PHP41.600, but the threat of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  should cap the dollar. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. dollar was well-bid against the New Taiwan dollar amid volatility in the local stock market. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. dollar was quoted at NT$30.758, up from NT$30.735 late Friday. Dealers said expect the pair to trade in narrow range of NT$30.700-NT$30.800 ahead of the May 20 presidential inauguration. \par \par \uc2  The Thai baht also was lower. The dollar was quoted at 38.975 baht, up from THB38.815 Friday. \par \par \uc2  The only \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to gain on the dollar was the South Korean won. The dollar was at 1,113.8 won, down from Friday's close of KRW1,114.50. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said they expect a range of KRW1,111-KRW1,116 Monday. \par \par \uc2  -By Alan Yonan Jr.; 65 421-4859; alan.yonan@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  1136 4250 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010805dw5f02e3d\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
