\par\par \b \uc2  Indonesia Govt Seems In Disarray Amid Market Slide \b0\par\par\uc2  By Grainne McCarthy \par \uc2 914 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 Mai 2000\par 11:55\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2000, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  JAKARTA -(Dow Jones)- A barrage of confusing and contradictory remarks from top Indonesian policy makers have sent shivers through the nation's markets, as analysts worry that the government is in disarray and unable to tackle its formidable economic challenges. \par \par \uc2  Indonesian markets fell again Friday after the government and \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  sent conflicting signals about what, if anything, they plan to do about a sharp drop in the rupiah. \par \par \uc2  That followed an extraordinary warning Thursday from senior Indonesian economics minister Kwik Kian Gie about the risks of investing in the country, given its continuing political instability. \par \par \uc2  Kwik, in an interview with Dow Jones Newswires, also implicitly criticized his boss, President Abdurrahman Wahid, by saying he - unlike Wahid - would be ashamed to go on foreign trips to entice foreign investment into the country, given the risks involved. He also signaled that he was unhappy with the recent appointment by Wahid of some other economic advisers, prompting speculation that the minister's days in the cabinet are numbered. \par \par \uc2  Analysts say that the confusing comments, apparent bickering and market gyrations have sparked a sense of panic about the management of economic policy in Indonesia. \par \par \uc2  "I think this is another clear sign of a lack of unity in the cabinet," said Raden Pardede, chief economist at Danareksa Securities in Jakarta. \par \par \uc2  "If the government doesn't make some tough decisions now to regain confidence of the market, the rupiah could deteriorate further very quickly," he added. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Along with the impact of the surging dollar, the comments sent the\par rupiah sinking to IDR8,768 during Friday trade, its lowest level since\par Sept. 23 and down 8% since Wednesday's close of Asian trade. Jakarta\par stocks dipped 3.5% Thursday and a further 0.4% Friday.\par -\par Lack Of Clarity Hits Economic Progress\par -\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Although there are some signs of recovery in Indonesia - Kwik said\par Thursday the economy grew 5% in the first two months of the year from a\par year ago - Indonesia's multilateral lenders and private economists\par reckon the economy won't recover in a sustainable way until Wahid's team\par tackles deeply embedded structural issues such as the mountain of\par private corporate debt and corruption at the courts.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par But a lack of a clear, united approach to revamping the economy is\par overriding any progress the government has already made.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par For example, in a bid to stem the rupiah's hemorrhaging, Bank\par Indonesia senior deputy governor Anwar Nasution said Wednesday that the\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  was ready to step into the market to shore up the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The next day, Kwik said that \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervening\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  would be fruitless, as\par battling psychological factors in the market could swiftly lead to a\par depletion of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reserves. Another \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  deputy\par governor then shot back later Thursday with a pledge that Bank Indonesia\par would use "all possible means," to protect the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par But on Friday, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  governor Sjahril Sabirin said Bank\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Indonesia\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  would only \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  "if it will be effective," backing up a\par widely-held market view that the bank was hoping words would be\par sufficient to talk the rupiah up, without having to resort to deeds.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Wahid himself has fueled the confusion. Earlier Friday, he said that\par the government had no quick fix for the rupiah, saying it would wait a\par week before deciding whether to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . But several hours later, he\par said that the rupiah was sliding due to fears about unrest over the\par weekend as protesters mark the second anniversary of riots that shook\par Jakarta in May 1998.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par If the rupiah remains weak on Monday, Wahid said the government would\par act, although he didn't say in what manner.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "We will wait until May 15," he said. "If the dollar doesn't weaken,\par then the government will take action"," he added, in comments that\par helped give the rupiah a pre-weekend lift towards the close of trade\par sending it to IDR8,300.\par -\par Growing Sense Of Panic\par -\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Many market participants believe Wahid is planning to further cull his\par cabinet - in the wake of last month's firing of two economic ministers -\par and Kwik is increasingly seen as next on the list. Wahid's recent\par appointment of two controversial officials to monitor and supervise\par Kwik's team has helped drive that speculation.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "One way or another, it looks like he is going to vacate his\par portfolio," said Philip Wee, treasury economist at Standard Chartered in\par Singapore.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par This, political analysts say, could pile further pressure on Wahid's\par coalition government and would make it increasingly difficult for the\par Democratic Party of Struggle - in which Kwik is a senior member and of\par which Vice-President Megawati Sukarnoputri is chairperson - to remain in\par the cabinet.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par But analysts also say Wahid needs to rein in his economic team, and if\par he does replace Kwik he should do so swiftly, rather than waiting until\par he delivers a key performance speech to parliament in August.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "Statements from politicians are confusing and marking the market\par nervous," said Pande Raja Silalahi, an economist at the Center for\par Strategic and International Studies in Jakarta.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "Now is the right time for the president to reorganize the\par cabinet...so he can concentrate on economic issues and make a solid\par team," he said. "He should not wait until August. Sacking Kwik may spark\par some reaction on the market but it won't last long and it would be\par better than all this uncertainty."\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par -By Grainne McCarthy; 62 21 3983 1277; gmccarthy@ap.org\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010805dw5c02cs3\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
