\par\par \b \uc2  Indonesian Min: BNI Loans Reveals Top Level Corruption \b0\par\par\uc2 758 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 29 November 1999\par 10:36\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1999, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  JAKARTA -(Dow Jones)- Indonesia's scandal-ridden banking sector was hit Monday by revelations that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  and former President Suharto were involved in high-level corruption related to the handling of 9.65 trillion rupiah ($1=IDR7,310) in loans by a state bank to an influential conglomerate. \par \par \uc2  Laksamana Sukardi, Minister of investment and state-enterprises, told Parliament that the controversy surrounding PT Bank Negara Indonesia, or BNI's, hefty loans to the Texmaco Group was "a case of high-level conspiracy." \par \par \uc2  The so-called conspiracy dated back to late 1997 after the rupiah suffered from hefty depreciation and had seriously impaired the financial positions of most Indonesian conglomerates. \par \par \uc2  Laksamana revealed to parliament that Bank Indonesia - Indonesia's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  - provided a total of $754.1 million plus IDR1.9 trillion in pre-shipment trade facility exclusively to diversified conglomerate Texmaco, contravening \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  regulations that only allow post-shipment facilities. \par \par \uc2  Texmaco faced increasing financial strain as the rupiah depreciated, and there were about $350 million in commercial papers and Yankee bonds that had matured at that time but couldn't be rolled over. \par \par \uc2  Then President Suharto even personally \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to instruct Bank \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia\b0 \highlight\uc2  through a letter to extend the facility and flout existing rules, Laksamana said. \par \par \uc2  The owner of Texmaco Group, Marimutu Sinivasan, is a close friend of Suharto. \par \par \uc2  "The pre-shipment facility was tailor-made for Texmaco and is a case of national-level collusion," Laksamana said. \par \par \uc2  Further, Laksamana said Bank Indonesia tapped into the country's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves to provide Texmaco with the loan facilities, at a time when the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was facing serious devaluation pressure. \par \par \uc2  "Bank Indonesia used the country's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves to bail out a local company at a time when Indonesia is in dire need of foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," he said. \par \par \uc2  Also, the amount of credit facility channeled through BNI to Texmaco had also breached the legal lending limit prescribed by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which stipulates that no bank can lend more than 20% of its capital to a single business group, he said. \par \par \uc2  However, Bank Indonesia granted an exemption to this rule to allow the facility to go through to Texmaco. \par \par \uc2  Laksamana added that Suharto's interference in Bank Indonesia's \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  was very clear. \par \par \uc2  Even the ministry of trade and industry at that time turned a blind eye to the fact that only Texmaco Group benefited from the pre-shipment facility, though the ministry had recommended two other business groups to be eligible for the facility. \par \par \uc2  The collusion trail didn't stop after the facilities were granted. A local media report disclosed and BNI subsequently confirmed that it didn't report its loan exposure to Texmaco Group to the Indonesian Bank Restructuring Agency, or IBRA. \par \par \uc2  IBRA was formed early last year to reform the shattered banking sector and BNI is one of the banks tagged for government recapitalization. \par \par \uc2  Under the recapitalization program, BNI should have transferred the worst portion of its non-performing loans to IBRA for recovery as IBRA has been given immense legal power to tackle recalcitrant debtors. \par \par \uc2  Despite Texmaco's failure to pay back any of the loans that led BNI to write off the loans completely last year, BNI didn't transfer the loans to IBRA and retain the loans on its books. \par \par \uc2  Most banking analysts reckon that such loans should have been transferred to IBRA for more effective collection. \par \par \uc2  "It's a big question mark as to why BNI didn't transfer the loan to IBRA, I think BNI is trying to avoid letting the powerful IBRA to be Texmaco's creditor," said Pradjoto, a banking analyst, and the whistle-blower of the on-going Bank Bali scandal. \par \par \uc2  If the loans were transferred, Texmaco Group would probably top the list as one of the largest debtors of IBRA, which has expressed determination to take stern steps against non-cooperative debtors. \par \par \uc2  BNI said in a press statement that it's negotiating with Texmaco on debt-restructuring proposal, which would include a debt-to-equity conversion. \par \par \uc2  The BNI scandal is the latest to hit the Indonesian banking sector. Since late July, the sector has been embroiled in the high-profiled Bank Bali scandal that involved the mis-use of funds under the government guarantee program, allegedly for political purposes. \par \par \uc2  And banking analysts say the BNI case is only one of possibly many collusion cases between state banks and powerful conglomerates \par \par \uc2  -By Noel Fung and I Made Sentana; 62 21 3983 1277; nfung@ap.org \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010826dvbt084cc\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
