\par\par \b \uc2  Key points of Indonesia bank scandal audit summary. \b0\par\par\uc2 942 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 September 1999\par 08:16\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  JAKARTA, Sept 16 (Reuters) - Following are key points of a summary prepared by PricewaterhouseCoopers (PwC) of its audit of transactions in Indonesia's Bank Bali scandal. \par \par \uc2  The auditors produced a full report of more than 100 pages and a 36-page summary. The former has been kept confidential, but copies of the summary are circulating among legislators and financial officials in Jakarta and Reuters has obtained a copy. \par \par \uc2  In a letter accompanying the summary, PwC said it "does not include all of the facts uncovered by our investigation". In particular, it leaves out analysis of the flow of funds. \par \par \uc2  All those who have been named in connection with the Bank Bali affair have denied wrongdoing. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  says it regards the summary of the audit as unfair and speculative. \par \par \uc2  THE BANK BALI SCANDAL: The scandal concerns a payment of 546 billion rupiah ($68 million) by PT Bank Bali Tbk to PT Era Giat Prima (EGP) for the recovery of loans from the Indonesian Bank Restructuring Agency (IBRA). EGP is run by Setya Novanto, deputy treasurer of the ruling Golkar party. \par \par \uc2  The Bank Bali loans were made to banks which were later taken over or shut down by IBRA. Bank Bali tried to get the loans repaid for months under a government guarantee scheme, without success. But after Novanto's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Bank \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia\b0 \highlight\uc2  and IBRA authorised the payment of 904 billion rupiah to Bank Bali. \par \par \uc2  CONCLUSIONS: PwC says it "uncovered numerous indicators of fraud, noncompliance, irregularity, misappropriation, undue preferential treatment, concealment, bribery and corruption during the processing and payment of the Bank Bali claims". \par \par \uc2  It urges further investigation of the potential involvement of several people, including "ministers, senior government officials, members of parliament, political party officials and prominent members of the business community". \par \par \uc2  The audit concludes Bank Bali's claims for loan repayment were regarded as ineligible, but were nevertheless paid. \par \par \uc2  "Overwhelming amounts of evidence came to hand from Bank Indonesia and IBRA which evidenced their jointly held view that the Bank Bali claims were administratively ineligible," it says. \par \par \uc2  OPPORTUNITY FOR FRAUD: The audit says there was considerable uncertainty over whether Indonesia's government would pay interbank claims, and such knowledge therefore "would constitute extremely valuable insider information," allowing those in the know to buy interbank claims in the market at a large discount. \par \par \uc2  It says during the period when Bank Bali was approached, "a small circle of government officials was aware of international financial institution pressure to settle interbank claims and the Indonesian government's decision to make payments". \par \par \uc2  This small circle included the IBRA executive committee and Bank Indonesia's board of governors, the summary said. \par \par \uc2  THE BANK BALI TRANSACTION: The summary notes a "sudden reversal" in the handling of Bank Bali's claims by Bank Indonesia and IBRA. After originally saying the claims were ineligible, both institutions later paid them. \par \par \uc2  It names an individual at IBRA who personally sought to get the claims repaid, despite concerns of others in IBRA. It says Bank Indonesia also appeared to give the Bank Bali claims preferential treatment. \par \par \uc2  It notes reports of several meetings between Bank Bali and top financial and political officials. Some of these meetings have been confirmed by the participants, others denied. \par \par \uc2  Rudy Ramli, president director of Bank Bali when the deals were done, has produced a journal detailing various meetings with top officials and close associates of President B.J. Habibie. The audit says a confidential source has confirmed Ramli's account and its probe found nothing to dispute its substance. \par \par \uc2  The summary says that after the payment by Bank Indonesia of Bank Bali's 904 billion rupiah claim on June 1, there was an "immediate explosion of financial transactions...involving numerous individuals and entities". \par \par \uc2  "These transactions involve techniques commonly associated with money laundering, including \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  swaps, \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  transactions and cash withdrawals," it says. \par \par \uc2  LIMITS OF AUDIT: PwC was appointed to investigate the Bank Bali transactions after pressure from the International Monetary Fund and World Bank for a transparent, independent audit. \par \par \uc2  But the summary details several limitations. "PwC has not been afforded adequate time and sufficient access to information, and has been delayed from conducting an investigation which meets international standards and which ensures complete transparency and accountability to the public," it said. \par \par \uc2  "However, the restrictions that were placed on us were not so severe as to prevent us from reaching conclusions on key issues." \par \par \uc2  PwC says that at the request of the Supreme Audit Agency, its summary does not disclose the names of the ultimate recipients of the 546 billion rupiah commission paid by Bank Bali to EGP. \par \par \uc2  It says its investigations were hampered by the refusal of various parties to comply with requests for key documents and Bank Indonesia's refusal to allow access to some bank accounts. \par \par \uc2  It said there was an "overwhelming impression that certain parties were interested (whether from instruction or of their own volition) to ensure that our review did not determine the ultimate recipient of funds out of the claim payment". \par \par \uc2  DIVISIONS IN IBRA: The audit summary notes tensions between high-level officials in IBRA. It says these disputes led to a refusal to hire staff for certain divisions and raised concerns about the impact on IBRA's \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  RISK OF RECURRENCE: The audit summary says "the current set up of the government guarantee scheme in IBRA and Bank Indonesia is not adequate to avoid a similar occurence". It recommends that IBRA develop a comprehensive fraud risk management programme. ((Andrew Marshall, Jakarta newsroom +62 21 384 6364, Fax +62 21 344 8404, jakarta.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010907dv9g00kc9\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
