\par\par \b \uc2  PRESS DIGEST-Indonesian business news - Sept 8. \b0\par\par\uc2 360 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 September 1999\par 03:22\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  The following are the leading stories in the main Indonesian business newspapers on Sept 8. Reuters has not verified these stories and does not vouch for their accuracy. \par \par \uc2  Telephone: Editorial, (6221) 384-6364. Fax:(6221) 344-8404 or Help Desk, (6221) 344-8406. Fax: (6221) 381-0862 \par \par \uc2  - - - - \par \par \uc2  BISNIS INDONESIA \par \par \uc2  - AUSTRALIA THREATENS TO BOYCOTT INDONESIAN CARGO \par \par \uc2  Australia's worker union said it would boycott cargo from Indonesia's airline Garuda Indonesia and from Indonesian ships starting from Wednesday to protest against violence in East Timor. \par \par \uc2  - RETURN OF FUNDS TO BANK BALI WAS TRANSFERED IN THE COUNTRY \par \par \uc2  Indonesia's Supreme Audit Agency and PriceWaterhouseCooper found evidence that the money returned to Bank Bali was transfered from a bank inside the country, not from overseas, a source close to the investigation says. \par \par \uc2  - - - - \par \par \uc2  HARIAN EKONOMI NERACA \par \par \uc2  - \b \highlight22\uc2 CENTRAL BANK INTERVENTION\b0 \highlight\uc2  FAILS TO PROP UP RUPIAH \par \par \uc2  Bank \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia\b0 \highlight\uc2  through several state banks has been trying to prop up the rupiah by \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market but so far it has failed to hold the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . It falls to 8,250/8,300 to the dollar. \par \par \uc2  - TIMOR RESISTANCE LEADER GUSMAO HIDES IN BRITISH EMBASSY \par \par \uc2  Timor resistance leader Xanana Gusmao takes refuge in the British Embassy until the security situation in East Timor improves. - - - - \par \par \uc2  THE JAKARTA POST \par \par \uc2  - EAST TIMOR VIOLENCE PUTS IMF BAILOUT AT RISK \par \par \uc2  The Australian government reiterates that withdrawing its support for a $43 billion IMF-led bailout for Indonesia is a last-resort option if Jakarta refuses to allow an international peacekeeping force in East Timor. \par \par \uc2  - INDONESIA MAY KEEP SHARE OF TIMOR GAP SALES \par \par \uc2  Indonesia could retain a share of oil and gas revenue from the area between Timor Island and Australia, known as Timor Gap, after East Timor's independence, says Sutomo Sudomo, technical director of the Australian Indonesian Joint Authority for the Timor Gap Zone Cooperation. \par \par \uc2  - - - - \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Looking for more information from local sources? Reuters Business Briefing has 11 Indonesian sources including Bisnis Indonesia, the Jakarta Post, and Kompas. For details, please call your local help desk \tab \tab \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par ((Jakarta newsroom +6221 384-6364; Fax +6221 344-8404, jakarta.newsroom@reuters.com)).\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (C) Reuters Limited 1999.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010907dv980122i\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
